@page "/learn/async-form-validation"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Async validation · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Async validation</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">Is this supplier code already taken? Only the database knows. Can your form wait for it?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Forms · Interactive Server · about 6 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> validation attributes can be async. <code>EditForm</code> waits for every check
        before it saves, and tells you while one is running. In .NET 10, validation rules couldn't be async, so a
        database check had to run outside validation, and Save didn't wait for it.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a <i>New supplier</i> form. A supplier code must look like <code>ABCD-01</code>, and no other
        supplier can have it. <code>EXOT-01</code> already belongs to Exotic Liquids.
    </p>
    <p>
        Only the database can check that second rule. Here it takes 1.5 seconds on purpose, like a busy server. This
        form does the check the .NET 10 way: in code that runs when the field changes.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The code check, in a field-changed handler" Url="demo/async-validation/suppliers/new/net10"
                Label=".NET 10: a field-changed handler" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            <p>
                <b>Try 1: save quickly.</b> Type any company, type <code>EXOT-01</code> as the <b>Supplier code</b>,
                and click <b>Save supplier</b> within a second. Save goes ahead, and only the database stops the
                duplicate: a red banner shows its error. "Already used" turns up under the field a moment later.
            </p>
            <p>
                <b>Try 2: change it quickly.</b> Press <b>Start over</b>. Type <code>EXOT-01</code> and press Tab.
                Within a second, change it to <code>ZZZZ-99</code> and press Tab. Wait two seconds: "already used"
                appears under <code>ZZZZ-99</code>, a code nobody has. It's the answer meant for <code>EXOT-01</code>.
            </p>
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>Save doesn't wait for the check</h2>
    <p>Here's what happens when you leave the field and press Save straight away:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">One quick Save, in order</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">0.0 s</span>  leave Supplier code  lookup starts    <span class="dim">nobody awaits it</span>
<span class="dim">0.1 s</span>  press Save           attributes run   <span class="ok">✓ all pass</span>
<span class="dim">0.1 s</span>  OnValidSubmit        inserts EXOT-01  <span class="err">unique index throws</span>
<span class="dim">1.5 s</span>  lookup answers       "already used"   <span class="err">Save already ran</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        In .NET 10, validation can't wait for anything. <code>EditForm</code> runs the attributes on the model, and
        none of them talks to the database. So the lookup runs in an <code>async void</code> handler, and Save can't
        wait for that.
    </p>
    <p>
        There's a second problem. A new edit starts a new lookup but doesn't stop the old one. When the old one
        answers, its message lands on whatever the field holds by then.
    </p>
    <p>
        In Fig. 1 a unique index in the database happens to catch the duplicate, so the user sees a database error
        instead of a message on the field. A rule with nothing like that behind it, such as "this VAT number must be
        registered", just lets the bad value save.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>An async attribute</h2>
    <p>Derive from <code>AsyncValidationAttribute</code>, and put it on the property like any other rule.</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip">public sealed class UniqueSupplierCodeAttribute()
    : <span class="hl">AsyncValidationAttribute</span>("{0} is already used by another supplier.")
{
    protected override async Task&lt;ValidationResult?&gt; <span class="hl">IsValidAsync</span>(
        object? value, ValidationContext context, <span class="hl">CancellationToken cancellationToken</span>)
    { <span class="dim">... ask the database ...</span> }
}

[Required, RegularExpression("^[A-Z]{4}-[0-9]{2}$")]
<span class="hl">[UniqueSupplierCode]</span>
public string? Code { get; set; }

<span class="dim">// The page: redraw when a check ends</span>
<span class="hl">form.OnValidationStateChanged += (_, _) =&gt; InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);</span>

@@if (<span class="hl">form.IsValidationPending(() =&gt; Input.Code)</span>) { &lt;span&gt;Checking…&lt;/span&gt; }
&lt;button type="submit" disabled="<span class="hl">@@form.IsValidationPending()</span>"&gt;</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        <code>EditForm</code> now waits for every check before it calls <code>OnValidSubmit</code>. Blazor only
        redraws the page after an event, like a click or a keypress. An async check can finish later than that, so
        the page listens to <code>OnValidationStateChanged</code> and redraws when the check finishes. Without that line, "Checking…" and the
        message don't show until something else redraws the page.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>IsValidationPending</code> works two ways. Give it a field, and it's true while that field's check
        runs: that drives the "Checking…" beside the box. Give it nothing, and it's true only while Save is waiting
        for the whole form. That's what disables the button: it greys out when you click Save, and comes back when
        every check has answered. Tabbing out of the field doesn't disable it.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same form, with an async attribute" Url="demo/async-validation/suppliers/new"
                Label=".NET 11: an async attribute">
        <Caption>
            <p>
                <b>Try 1 again.</b> Type a company and <code>EXOT-01</code>, then click <b>Save supplier</b> straight
                away. The button greys out and says <b>Checking…</b> for about a second and a half. Then the field says
                "Supplier code is already used by another supplier." Nothing saves.
            </p>
            <p>
                <b>Try 2 again.</b> Press <b>Start over</b>, type <code>EXOT-01</code> and Tab, then quickly change it
                to <code>ZZZZ-99</code> and Tab. "Checking…" shows, then clears with no message: the newer edit
                cancelled the old check.
            </p>
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Here are the files behind both demos:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/AsyncValidation/UniqueSupplierCodeAttribute.cs" Feature="async-form-validation">
        <Caption>
            The attribute in Fig. 2. It gets the database from the <code>ValidationContext</code>, and passes the
            cancellation token along so a newer edit can cancel it.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/AsyncValidation/NewSupplierForm.cs" Feature="async-form-validation">
        <Caption>
            The model. The async rule sits with the others, and only runs once they pass. Fig. 1 posts
            <code>NewSupplierFormNet10</code>, the same fields without <code>[UniqueSupplierCode]</code>.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="5" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/AsyncValidation/NewSupplier.razor" Feature="async-form-validation">
        <Caption>
            The page in Fig. 2: "Checking…" beside the field, and a Save button that waits for the whole form.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="6" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/AsyncValidation/NewSupplierNet10.razor" Feature="async-form-validation">
        <Caption>
            The page in Fig. 1: the same lookup, in a handler nothing waits for.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>The check lived outside validation</h2>
    <p>There were three places to put a database check, and each had a cost:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Three ways to check a code in .NET 10</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">async Task SaveAsync(EditContext form)
{
    if (<span class="hl">await IsTakenAsync(Input.Code)</span>)
    { messages.Add(...); return; }</pre>
                <span class="why">check again in the save handler</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ the rule lives away from the others, and nobody hears about it until Save</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">form.OnFieldChanged += <span class="err">async</span> (_, e) =&gt;
{
    if (await IsTakenAsync(Input.Code))
        messages.Add(...);
};</pre>
                <span class="why">check in a field-changed handler, as Fig. 1 does</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ Save doesn't wait for it, and an old answer can land on a newer value</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">protected override ValidationResult? IsValid(...)
    =&gt; IsTakenAsync(code)<span class="err">.GetAwaiter().GetResult()</span> ...</pre>
                <span class="why">block on the lookup in a plain attribute</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ freezes the page: no message, and the form stops responding</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        Most apps used the first two together: the handler for early feedback, and the save handler to catch what it
        missed. That fixes Try 1. Try 2's wrong message stays, and the rule is now written twice, both in the page instead of on the model.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>Validation that can wait</h2>
    <p>
        <code>AsyncValidationAttribute</code> is new in <code>System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations</code>.
        <code>DataAnnotationsValidator</code> runs it with the other rules: when the field changes, and again before
        Save. That's what Fig. 2 runs. It also works on a non-interactive (static) form: the server runs the check
        when the form is posted, and waits for it before saving.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>Name the field in the result.</b> Pass <code>[context.MemberName!]</code> to <code>ValidationResult</code>, as Fig. 3 does. Without it, the check Save runs gives a message tied to no field. No <code>ValidationMessage</code> shows it, and the form just won't save.</li>
        <li><b>Marked models name it for you.</b> On a model marked <code>[ValidatableType]</code> (see <a href="learn/validatable-type-stable">Nested validation</a>), you can skip this. This demo's model isn't marked.</li>
        <li><b>Override <code>IsValid</code> too.</b> You have to, because it's abstract. Throwing is fine, as Fig. 3 does, since Blazor only calls <code>IsValidAsync</code>.</li>
        <li><b>Calling <code>Validate()</code> yourself?</b> Switch to <code>await form.ValidateAsync()</code>. <code>EditContext.Validate()</code> is obsolete in .NET 11, and throws once there's an async check.</li>
        <li><b>A check that crashes isn't the same as a failed one.</b> If a check throws, the field gets no message. Instead <code>IsValidationFaulted()</code> is true and the form won't submit, so show a banner saying so.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
